+## D21 — Reachability is validated analytically, not numerically
+
+Workspace questions are settled with **EAIK** closed-form IK, not a numerical
+solver. A closed-form solver returning no exact solution is a statement about
+the workspace; a numerical failure is ambiguous. Rules that go with it:
+
+- **Reject every `is_LS_vec` solution.** Least-squares results are the nearest
+  reachable pose, not the requested one, and accepting them turns every
+  unreachable pose into a false positive.
+- **Re-verify every solution through `fwdKin`** against the request.
+- **Joint limits come from the URDF**, never the USD, whose blanket ±360° would
+  hide real violations.
+- **Keep the frame cross-check blocking.** The URDF flange frame is identity at
+  zero; the USD's is `[[1,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0]]`. Targets convert as
+  `R_urdf = R_usd @ Aᵀ`. The first sweep run was wrong by 90° and produced a
+  *plausible* answer; only the self-check caught it. Validate off-singularity —
+  the obvious `IK(FK(0)) == 0` test fails legitimately, because q=0 is the
+  fully-extended singular configuration.
+
+EAIK is installed **locally** as well as on the program host, so the full
+reachability sweep runs on the development machine in seconds. An earlier note
+claiming it was program-host-only was wrong and cost several remote round-trips.
+
+## D22 - A check that re-derives its subject proves nothing
+
+`config.min_rack_width` carried the docstring *"Mirrors `_min_rack_width` in
+`build_env/env_config_generator.py`; if that changes, this must change with it
+or racks will be generated too narrow."* It did not mirror it: it allowed a jig
+gap at both ends where the generator allows one, so it ran 16 mm wide for every
+rack. The prediction in its own docstring came true.
+
+It survived because **both guards checked the model against itself**:
+
+- `test_minimum_rack_width_matches_the_environment_generator` was named for the
+  generator and asserted two transcribed literals, `0.7440` and `1.0880`. It
+  never called the generator. The literals were simply the wrong formula's
+  output, so the test agreed with the bug.
+- `test_every_shelf_is_wide_enough_for_its_jigs` recomputed the built width with
+  `cell_core`'s own formula instead of reading the generator's output, so it
+  confirmed an arithmetic identity rather than a fact about the stage.
+
+The consequence was not the 16 mm. `_size_x_margin` takes the max over racks, so
+a uniform error would have cancelled. The error decided **which rack won the
+max**: the margin came out 0.072 instead of 0.088, the outgoing shelf was built
+at 1.144 m against a measured 1.160 m, and `unsupported()` reported the wrong
+rack in the wrong direction. A shelf built *narrower* than the one measured
+flatters every clearance result computed against it, and the fidelity report
+pointed at the shelf that was fine.
+
+Rules:
+
+- **A test that asserts a value derived from the thing under test is an
+  identity, not a check.** Cross-boundary claims must call across the boundary.
+- **Never transcribe a formula.** D18 already forbade transcribing numbers; a
+  formula is worse, because it reproduces the error at every input.
+- **Sweep the inputs when pinning agreement.** A single-point comparison can
+  pass on a coincidence; agreement was pinned over jig counts 1-8 and several
+  gaps and radii, so any edit to either formula must be made to both.
+- **Assert the direction that is unsafe.** Wider than measured is conservative
+  and reportable; narrower is silently optimistic. That asymmetry is now a
+  test of its own.
+
