+# Findings 03 — Real rack setup, tray underside, and the revised clearance budget
+
+Date: 2026-09-22. Inputs: `todo/resources/isaacsim/real_rack_setup.md` (user),
+tray mass 1.2 kg (user), plus USD mesh analysis on the isaacsim host.
+
+Supersedes the rack-layout and D2-clearance sections of `findings/02`.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Generator defaults — now pinned
+
+`create_environment()` does **not** pass `plane_thickness` or `cylinder_radius`
+to `create_rack_shelf_with_tray_jig()`, so the function defaults apply:
+
+| Parameter | Value | Source |
+|---|---|---|
+| `plane_thickness` | **0.020** | `create_rack_shelf_with_tray_jig` default |
+| `cylinder_radius` | **0.020** | same; matches `_CYLINDER_RADIUS` in the generator |
+| `rack_depth` | 0.36 (sim) | `_RACK_DEPTH` |
+| `JIG_X_HALF` | 0.078 | shared constant |
+| `JIG_Y_OFFSET` | 0.135 | shared constant |
+
+**Correction to `findings/02 §7`:** with `plane_thickness = 0.020` (not 0.030),
+the jig origin sits at shelf-centre `+0.010`, so the label spans world
+`z ∈ [h − 0.020, h + 0.010]` and the **label centre is at `h − 0.005`**, not `h`.
+
+Derived stack for a shelf plane at height `h` (thickness `t = 0.020`):
+
+| Feature | World z |
+|---|---|
+| Shelf plate | `h − 0.010 → h + 0.010` |
+| Label (AprilGrid carrier) | `h − 0.020 → h + 0.010`, centre `h − 0.005` |
+| Jig base plate | `h + 0.010 → h + 0.015` |
+| Jig columns | `h + 0.015 → h + 0.045` |
+| Tray | `h + 0.045 → h + 0.130` |
+
+## 2. `size_x` / `size_y` — an ambiguity I must not guess
+
+`create_environment` documents rack size as `(size_x, size_y, size_z)` with
+defaults `right = (0.61, 0.36, 1.0)`, `left = (1.12, 0.36, 1.2)`. So in code,
+**`size_x` = width (the face the jigs are arrayed along), `size_y` = depth**.
+
+`real_rack_setup.md` states `size_y = 1.160 / 0.800` and `size_x = 0.308`, which
+is the **opposite** assignment. Two readings:
+
+| | Reading A (literal) | Reading B (physical) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Width | 0.308 | **1.160** out / **0.800** in |
+| Depth | 1.160 / 0.800 | **0.308** |
+| Jigs per layer | **1** | **6** out / **4** in |
+| Total rack slots | 3 + 4 = 7 | 12 + 24 = **36** |
+
+**Reading B is CONFIRMED by the user.** Width = 1.160 (outgoing) / 0.800
+(incoming); depth = 0.308. Three independent checks agreed beforehand:
+
+1. Depth 0.308 ≈ the sim default 0.36; a 1.16 m-deep rack holding a 0.27 m jig
+   would waste 0.9 m of depth.
+2. The widths fit a whole number of jigs *exactly* under the real `jig_gap = 0.016`:
+   `min_width(6) = 1.072 ≤ 1.160` (margin 0.088) and
+   `min_width(4) = 0.728 ≤ 0.800` (margin 0.072). Both margins are below the
+   0.172 step, so neither admits an extra column. Seven jigs would need 1.244.
+3. The scheduler rule "top to bottom, then left to right" only means something
+   with multiple columns per layer.
+
+## 3. Real rack layout (CONFIRMED)
+
+| | Incoming (right, +X) | Outgoing (left, −X) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Width (along world Y) | 0.800 | 1.160 |
+| Depth | 0.308 | 0.308 |
+| Layers | 3 | 4 |
+| Lowest layer z | 0.820 | 0.550 |
+| Layer gap | **0.250** | **0.250** |
+| Layer heights | **0.82, 1.07, 1.32** | **0.55, 0.80, 1.05, 1.30** |
+| Jigs per layer | 4 | 6 |
+| Slots | **12** | **24** |
+| Front face from robot origin | **0.792** | **0.792** |
+
+Plus 1 table slot → **37 addressable slots**.
+
+Layout: the racks sit on **opposite sides along world X**, rotated ±90° so their
+front faces point at the robot; their **width runs along world Y, centred on
+y = 0**. The table is at +Y. So jig lateral offsets `u` are world-Y offsets:
+
+- Incoming, 4 jigs, step 0.172 → `u ∈ {−0.258, −0.086, +0.086, +0.258}`
+- Outgoing, 6 jigs, step 0.172 → `u ∈ {−0.430, −0.258, −0.086, +0.086, +0.258, +0.430}`
+
+`jig_gap = 0.016` (sim default was 0.015).
+
+## 4. **The layer gap is 0.25 m, not 0.30 m — this kills the D2 mitigation**
+
+> **Revised 2026-09-22.** An earlier revision of this section put the interference
+> at 3 mm. That used `findings/02`'s EIH camera offset, which was **wrong by
+> ~44 mm**. The corrected figure is **41.6 mm**, and it changes the conclusion
+> from "needs a small camera raise" to "the 0.12 m close standoff is unusable".
+
+`findings/02 §9` validated the reduced-standoff sighting pose against the *sim*
+layer spacing of 0.30 m and reported 47 mm of clearance. The real spacing is
+**0.25 m**, and the camera offset it used was wrong.
+
+**Corrected camera offset.** The EIH camera sits at flange `+Y 0.11383`,
+`+Z 0.10670` — not `+Y 0.0752 / +Z 0.0442`. Two independent sources agree:
+
+- `tools/verify_flange_offsets.py` re-measured **all five** camera prims in
+  `tm5s.usd` (`rsd455_v1`, `Camera`, `Glass`, `Front_mask`, and the Glass mesh
+  extent). They agree at `fy = 0.11383, fz = 0.10670`.
+- The commented-out `joint_link_6-Camera` in `tm5s_v4.urdf` carries
+  `xyz="-0.0002088 0.113722 0.106752"` — a completely separate file, agreeing to
+  under 0.2 mm.
+
+With the plate bottom at flange `−0.04269`, the plate bottom is **`0.15652`**
+below the camera, not `0.1179`.
+
+Let `G` = layer gap, `Δ` = height of the EIH camera above the label centre:
+
+```
+clearance = (camera_z − 0.15652) − (tray_top_on_layer_below)
+          = G + Δ − 0.29159          <- plane_thickness cancels
+```
+
+| Case | `G` | `Δ` | Clearance |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Sim default, as reported in findings/02 | 0.300 | 0 | ~~+0.047~~ **+0.0084** |
+| **Real racks** | **0.250** | **0** | **−0.0416 (INTERFERENCE)** |
+| Real + 40 mm camera raise | 0.250 | 0.040 | −0.0016 (still interfering) |
+| Real + 62 mm camera raise | 0.250 | 0.062 | +0.020 |
+
+So at the real spacing the bottom plate **collides with the tray on the layer
+below by 41.6 mm**. Note the sim configuration was never comfortable either: its
+true clearance is 8.4 mm, not the 47 mm originally reported.
+
+### Why the fixes don't work
+
+- **Raising the camera no longer fits in the image.** Clearing the lower tray by
+  20 mm needs `Δ ≈ 0.062`. At 0.12 m standoff with config 2 (`fy = 2653`) the
+  vertical half-field is only `0.12 × 972 / 2653 = 0.0443 m`. **The label leaves
+  the frame before the plate clears the tray.** This is the fix the previous
+  revision recommended; it is dead.
+- **Pitching the camera down to re-centre the label is worse.** Re-centring a
+  62 mm offset at 0.12 m needs `θ = atan(0.062/0.12) = 27.3°`, which drops the
+  plate tip by `0.3185·sin(27.3°) = 0.146 m` — more than twice the clearance
+  gained.
+- **Increasing standoff does not change the clearance formula** — `Δ` is a pure
+  vertical offset in the flange frame and standoff cancels — **but far enough
+  out, the plate stops being in the shelf at all.** That is the escape hatch.
+
+### What does work: back the close read off
+
+Two thresholds, both computed in `tools/cell_geometry.py`:
+
+| standoff | meaning |
+|---|---|
+| **≥ 0.1783 m** | plate stops overhanging the tray on the layer below |
+| **≥ 0.2118 m** | plate never enters the shelf at all |
+
+Take **0.2118 m**. The accuracy cost is real but modest — the AprilGrid spans
+~1284 px instead of 2266 px at 0.12 m, still **1.65×** the far read's 777 px.
+The original 2.9× improvement is not available at the real layer gap.
+
+`findings/04 §4` confirms this is free in reachability terms: the sweep returns
+identical results at 0.19, 0.2118, 0.25 and 0.35 m standoff. The sighting poses
+are nowhere near the workspace boundary, so the standoff can be chosen purely on
+optical and collision grounds.
+
+### The clearance is no longer slot-dependent
+
+At 0.2118 m the plate is outside the shelf regardless of what is on the layer
+below, so the sighting pose **no longer depends on the occupancy of the slot
+below**. This retires the scheduler/world-model/look-pose coupling that the
+previous revision introduced (see D15, now superseded).
+
+## 5. Tray underside — **it is recessed by 2.50 mm** (measured)
+
+Measured by transforming all 214 896 tray mesh points into a bottom-referenced
+frame and taking minima over the plate's exact corridor.
+
+### Contact band
+
+Points at `z < 0.5 mm` occupy only tray-local `x ∈ [0, 0.0065]` and
+`x ∈ [0.1263, 0.1329]` — **two 6.5 mm rails along the long edges**, nothing else.
+The jig columns are 25 mm wide and underlie tray-local `x ∈ [0, 0.0135]` and
+`[0.1195, 0.1329]`, so only the **outer 6.5 mm of each column top actually bears
+load**. Contact patch ≈ 4 × 6.5 × 20 mm = 520 mm² for a 1.2 kg tray.
+
+### Recess depth over the plate corridor
+
+The 60 mm plate runs at tray-local `x ∈ [0.0364, 0.0964]`. Min underside height
+per 10 mm Y band:
+
+| Region | min z above rail plane |
+|---|---|
+| Both ends (`y < 0.010`, `y > 0.237`) | 6.51 mm |
+| Next bands in | 4.10 / 4.91 mm |
+| **Entire interior (`0.020 < y < 0.227`)** | **2.50 – 3.50 mm** |
+| **Binding value** | **2.50 mm** |
+
+The earlier coarse 12×20 grid reported 3–4 mm; that was a gridding artefact.
+**2.50 mm is the correct figure.**
+
+### The recess is much wider than the plate
+
+Min-z stays at exactly 2.50 mm out to a corridor half-width of **0.055** and
+only drops to 0 at 0.060. So the recess is ~110 mm wide against a 60 mm plate —
+**30 mm of lateral margin per side** before the plate could reach a rail.
+
+Cross-check: the lateral budget is ±0.023, set by the **jig columns**
+(clear pocket 0.106 − plate 0.060 = 0.046). Since 0.023 < 0.030, the columns
+bind and the rails never do. The two independent constraints are consistent.
+
+### Consequence: the pitch budget loosens
+
+The plate's vertical corridor runs from the jig base plate top (jig z 0.005) to
+the **recessed** underside (jig z `0.035 + 0.0025 = 0.0375`), not to the rail
+plane at 0.035:
+
+| Quantity | findings/02 | **Revised** |
+|---|---|---|
+| Corridor height | 0.0300 | **0.0325** |
+| Plate thickness | 0.0100 | 0.0100 |
+| Vertical slack | 0.0200 (±0.0100) | **0.0225 (±0.01125)** |
+| **Pitch budget** (over 0.225 engagement) | **2.55°** | **2.87°** |
+
+A 12 % relaxation of the binding constraint. Lateral (±0.023) and yaw (5.9°)
+are unchanged.
+
+Also: load transfer happens at the recess plane, so the plate travels 2.5 mm
+further up than a flat-bottom model predicts before it takes the tray's weight.
+The specified 0.04 m lift absorbs this.
+
+**Caveat for the real cell:** this is the *sim asset's* geometry. A 2.5 mm
+feature is exactly the kind of thing a CAD-to-USD export can round away or
+invent. Do not bank the extra 0.0025 until the physical tray is measured —
+keep 2.55° as the design figure and treat 2.87° as headroom.
+
+## 6. Carry dynamics — now computable (tray = 1.2 kg)
+
+With tilt `θ = 15°` and friction coefficient `μ` (still unknown):
+
+**Forward slide-off (the tray sliding out along the plate)** is the binding mode:
+
+```
+a_max = g (μ cos θ + sin θ)
+```
+
+| `μ` | `a_max` |
+|---|---|
+| 0.05 | 2.99 m/s² |
+| 0.10 | 3.49 m/s² |
+| 0.20 | 4.43 m/s² |
+
+The `sin θ = 0.259` term dominates — **the 15° tilt alone provides 2.54 m/s²**
+even with zero friction. That is a good design: retention degrades gracefully.
+
+**Lateral** is friction-only (`a ≤ μ g cos θ` ≈ 0.95 m/s² at μ = 0.1) and would
+be the binding mode — *except* that the jaws close to a width fit (D5), which
+constrains the tray mechanically. This is an additional, independent reason the
+width-fit close matters; it is not merely belt-and-braces.
+
+**Downward** acceleration unloads the plate; cap at `0.5 g` to retain half the
+normal force.
+
+Proposed `grasp_profile.yaml: carry_limits` (conservative, tunable per D4):
+
+```yaml
+carry_limits:
+  linear_velocity:      0.25   # m/s
+  linear_acceleration:  1.50   # m/s^2   (~2x margin on the mu=0.05 case)
+  angular_velocity:     0.50   # rad/s
+  angular_acceleration: 3.00   # rad/s^2 (~0.66 m/s^2 at the tray CoM, r~0.22)
+  max_downward_accel:   4.00   # m/s^2
+payload:
+  tray_mass: 1.2                # kg -> change_load placeholder (D5)
+```
+
+## 7. Reachability — bound by the INSERTION pose, and the top corners are marginal
+
+### Robot base height (from the user's edited scripts)
+
+`create_robot_arm_base` stacks: `base_sz` + `leg_height` + `plate_thickness`.
+With the user's `base_sz = 0.1765` (was 0.25), `leg_height = 0.47` (was 0.5) and
+the unchanged `plate_thickness = 0.03`:
+
+```
+arm_z (tm5s origin) = 0.1765 + 0.47 + 0.03 = 0.6765
+shoulder (joint_2)  = 0.6765 + 0.1484     = 0.8249
+```
+
+Note this is **0.1035 m lower** than the sim default (0.78 / 0.9284). That helps
+the 0.55 m outgoing layer and hurts the 1.32 m incoming layer.
+
+### Max reach
+
+The USD chain gives a joint‑ 2‑to‑flange distance of **0.988 m** at the straight-up
+zero config, above the TM5S-900's nominal 900 mm spec (the spec envelope is
+limited by joint limits and singularity avoidance, not raw link length).
+Treat **0.90 m as the usable radius** and 0.988 as the hard geometric ceiling.
+
+### The binding pose is deep insertion, not sighting
+
+| Pose | Flange distance from robot axis |
+|---|---|
+| Far sighting (0.35 m) | 0.376 |
+| Close sighting (0.12 m) | 0.606 |
+| **Full insertion** | **0.7235** |
+
+(label plane at `0.792 − 0.022 = 0.770`; insertion leaves the flange 0.0465 in
+front of it; flange z at insertion = `h + 0.06895`.)
+
+So `d(h, u) = sqrt(0.7235² + u² + (h − 0.75595)²)`.
+
+**Centre column (`u = 0`):**
+
+| Rack | `h` | `d` | |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Outgoing | 0.55 | 0.752 | ok |
+| Outgoing | 0.80 | 0.725 | ok |
+| Outgoing | 1.05 | 0.781 | ok |
+| Outgoing | **1.30** | **0.905** | **at the limit** |
+| Incoming | 0.82 | 0.726 | ok |
+| Incoming | 1.07 | 0.789 | ok |
+| Incoming | **1.32** | **0.917** | **over the 0.90 spec** |
+
+**Outermost column:**
+
+| Rack | `h` | `u` | `d` | |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Outgoing | 1.30 | 0.430 | **1.002** | **beyond geometric max** |
+| Outgoing | 1.05 | 0.430 | 0.898 | at the limit |
+| Incoming | 1.32 | 0.258 | **0.953** | **over spec** |
+| Incoming | 1.07 | 0.258 | 0.830 | ok |
+
+**Conclusion:** the **top layer of both racks is at or beyond the reach limit at
+insertion depth**, and the outgoing rack's top outer corners are unreachable
+outright. Sighting those same slots is easy (0.75–0.79 m) — the robot can *see*
+slots it cannot *service*.
+
+This is a distance-sphere argument only; it ignores the orientation constraint
+(the flange must point horizontally into the shelf, which consumes wrist DOF
+near full extension) and joint limits, both of which make it **worse**, not
+better. It must be settled by an actual IK sweep, not by this arithmetic — see
+the proposed reachability map.
+
+> **Settled 2026-09-22 by `findings/04`.** The analytic EAIK sweep confirms this
+> section's direction but is less pessimistic about it. Of 37 slots, **35 are
+> fully serviceable**; only `outgoing/L3/C0` and `outgoing/L3/C5` fail, and only
+> in the `insert` and `lift` phases — short by 13–36 mm. The incoming rack's top
+> layer, predicted "over spec" here, does solve (though `incoming/L2` lift clears
+> by only 11.8 mm). **Zero joint-limit violations.** Raising the robot base to
+> `origin_z ≥ 0.734` gives 37/37; `0.7765` is recommended for margin.
+
