@@ -0,0 +1,311 @@
+# 04 — IK Reachability Sweep (EAIK, analytic)
+
+**Date:** 2026-09-22
+**Tools:** `.agent-workspace/tools/cell_geometry.py`, `.agent-workspace/tools/reach_sweep.py`
+**Artifacts:** `.agent-workspace/scratch/sweep_asbuilt.json`, `.agent-workspace/scratch/sweep_recommended.json`
+**Host:** `program` (docker `rekep`) — EAIK 1.2.2 is installed there and nowhere else.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Headline
+
+| question | answer |
+|---|---|
+| Is the cell reachable as currently specified? | **Almost.** 218 / 222 poses, **35 / 37 slots**. |
+| What fails? | `outgoing/L3/C0` and `outgoing/L3/C5` — top shelf, outermost columns — in the **insert** and **lift** phases only. |
+| Is it a joint-limit problem? | **No.** Zero limit violations anywhere. It is pure workspace: the arm is ~13 mm too short. |
+| Cheapest fix? | Raise the robot base. **origin_z ≥ 0.734 m** gives 37/37; **0.7765 m** gives 37/37 with real margin. |
+| Does the corrected camera standoff cost reach? | **No.** Reachability is identical at near-standoff 0.19 / 0.2118 / 0.25 / 0.35 m. |
+| Is the 2-tray-deep pick reachable? | Not yet tested — insertion here means the *jig* pocket, back margin 10 mm. |
+
+**Recommendation: raise the robot mounting to `origin_z = 0.7765 m` (+100 mm on the current build) and keep the racks at the measured 0.792 m.** That configuration sweeps 222/222 with a 18 mm worst-case reach margin and lets the near-sighting standoff move to the 0.2118 m the corrected camera geometry requires.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Method
+
+### 1.1 Why analytic IK
+
+`EAIK` solves the TM5S in closed form. It reports the arm as kinematic family **`6R-THREE_INNER_PARALLEL`**, with `hasKnownDecomposition = True` and `hasSphericalWrist = False`. Closed form matters here: a numerical IK failure is ambiguous (bad seed? bad tolerance? genuinely unreachable?), whereas a closed-form solver that returns no exact solution is making a **statement about the workspace**. Every "unreachable" verdict below is a real geometric fact, not a solver artefact.
+
+Two guards were still necessary:
+
+- EAIK returns least-squares approximations alongside exact solutions, flagged in `is_LS_vec`. **All LS solutions are rejected.** An LS solution is the nearest reachable pose, not the requested one; accepting them would have turned every unreachable pose into a false positive.
+- Every surviving solution is re-verified through `fwdKin` against the requested pose (`1e-4 m`, `1e-3` on the rotation matrix). Nothing is trusted on the solver's word.
+
+Solutions are then wrapped by ±2π where that brings a joint inside its limit, and checked against the **URDF** joint limits.
+
+### 1.2 Joint limits — the URDF is the honest source
+
+The USD declares a blanket ±360° on every joint. The URDF carries the real TM5S limits, which are much tighter:
+
+| joint | limit (deg) |
+|---|---|
+| joint_1 | ±318.86 |
+| joint_2 | ±180.00 |
+| joint_3 | ±155.00 |
+| joint_4 | ±210.00 |
+| joint_5 | ±150.00 |
+| joint_6 | ±210.00 |
+
+The sweep uses the URDF values. As it turns out this did not bind anywhere — but had the USD values been used, a limit violation would have been invisible.
+
+### 1.3 A frame bug the script caught on itself
+
+The first sweep run printed a cross-check failure:
+
+```
+FK(q=0) vs tm5s.usd flange: dp=0.000132 m  dR=2.000000
+```
+
+Position agreed to 0.13 mm; orientation did not agree at all. The URDF flange frame is **identity at zero**, the USD flange frame is `[[1,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0]]`. Both describe the same physical flange, so they differ by a constant right-multiplication:
+
+$$A = R_{\text{urdf}}(0)^{\mathsf T}\,R_{\text{usd}}(0) = \begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\0&0&-1\\0&1&0\end{bmatrix}$$
+
+In plain terms: **USD flange +Y (up) = URDF +Z; USD +Z (approach) = URDF −Y; USD +X = URDF +X.**
+
+`cell_geometry.py` builds poses in the USD convention, so every target is converted as $R_{\text{urdf}} = R_{\text{usd}} A^{\mathsf T}$ before it reaches EAIK.
+
+**The first run's results were all wrong by 90° and were discarded.** They are recorded here only as a warning: that run produced a plausible-looking answer (192 ok / 30 unreachable, blocked slots clustered sensibly on the top layers, a monotone sensitivity table) that would have been accepted without complaint had the cross-check not been there. *Plausibility is not validation.*
+
+The validation is now permanent and blocking — the script exits non-zero rather than emit numbers:
+
+1. FK(0) position must match the USD flange within 1 mm.
+2. `A` must be orthonormal.
+3. A USD→URDF→USD convention round-trip over 12 random configurations must be exact to 0 (achieved: `0.000e+00`).
+4. IK must recover a pose generated by the robot's own FK at a **non-singular** configuration.
+
+Note on (4): the obvious test — IK the zero pose, expect `q = 0` — *fails*, and correctly so. `q = 0` is the fully-extended singular configuration, where the 0.13 mm URDF/USD flange origin discrepancy maps to ~1.4° of joint motion. The test was moved off the singularity rather than loosened.
+
+### 1.4 Residual model error
+
+The URDF and USD flange origins differ by **0.132 mm**. That is below every tolerance in play (the insertion corridor is ±10 mm) and is not corrected. It is recorded so it is not rediscovered later as a mystery.
+
+### 1.5 What was swept
+
+37 slots × 6 phases = **222 poses**.
+
+| slots | count |
+|---|---|
+| incoming rack (3 layers × 4 columns) | 12 |
+| outgoing rack (4 layers × 6 columns) | 24 |
+| inspection table | 1 |
+
+Phases, from `cell_geometry.phase_poses()`:
+
+| phase | what it is |
+|---|---|
+| `sight_far` | AprilGrid read at 0.35 m standoff |
+| `sight_near` | close read, 0.19 m in the as-built sweep |
+| `pre_insert` | gripper aligned, 10 mm short of the tray |
+| `insert` | plate fully under the tray, 10 mm from the shelf back |
+| `lift` | tray raised 40 mm off the jig |
+| `withdraw` | retracted clear of the shelf |
+
+`insert` and `lift` are the binding phases everywhere — they are the only ones that put the flange deep inside a shelf.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Results — as-built (`origin_z = 0.6765`, rack distance `0.792`, near standoff `0.19`)
+
+```
+verdict counts: ok=218, unreachable=4        (limit violations: 0)
+
+  slot                   sight_  sight_  pre_in  insert  lift    withdr
+  incoming/L0..L2 (all)     .       .       .       .       .       .
+  outgoing/L0..L2 (all)     .       .       .       .       .       .
+  outgoing/L3/C0           .       .       .       X       X       .
+  outgoing/L3/C1..C4       .       .       .       .       .       .
+  outgoing/L3/C5           .       .       .       X       X       .
+  table/L0/C0              .       .       .       .       .       .
+
+slots fully serviceable: 35 / 37
+```
+
+### 2.1 How short is short
+
+Geometric ceiling, shoulder (joint_2, z = base + 0.1484) to flange at q = 0: **0.9879 m**. (The TM5S-900 is nominally a 900 mm arm; the extra 88 mm is the gripper-free flange radius, and it is a *ceiling*, not a usable envelope.)
+
+| slot | phase | shoulder radius | short by |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| outgoing/L3/C0 | insert | 1.0011 | **13.2 mm** |
+| outgoing/L3/C0 | lift | 1.0234 | **35.5 mm** |
+| outgoing/L3/C5 | insert | 1.0011 | 13.2 mm |
+| outgoing/L3/C5 | lift | 1.0234 | 35.5 mm |
+
+Tightest poses that *do* solve:
+
+| slot | phase | shoulder radius | margin |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| incoming/L2/C0 | lift | 0.9761 | **11.8 mm** |
+| incoming/L2/C3 | lift | 0.9761 | 11.8 mm |
+| outgoing/L3/C1 | lift | 0.9638 | 24.1 mm |
+| incoming/L2/C0 | insert | 0.9518 | 36.1 mm |
+
+So the as-built configuration is not "35/37 with room to spare" — it is **35/37 with 11.8 mm to spare**. Two slots are over the line and two more are within a centimetre of it. This is not a configuration to build.
+
+(Caveat: shoulder radius vs. the q=0 ceiling is a proxy. The true envelope is not a sphere — the wrist offsets make it lumpy, which is why at `origin_z = 0.734` a pose with −3.8 mm of nominal "margin" still solves. EAIK's verdict is authoritative; the margin column is for intuition and for ranking configurations.)
+
+---
+
+## 3. Sensitivity — base height × rack distance
+
+Fully-serviceable slots out of 37:
+
+| origin_z ↓ / rack dist → | 0.700 | 0.750 | **0.792** | 0.850 | 0.900 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.5765 | 21 | 31 | 31 | 25 | 25 |
+| 0.6265 | 23 | 35 | 35 | 29 | 25 |
+| **0.6765** *(as built)* | 23 | 35 | **35** | 31 | 25 |
+| 0.7265 | 23 | 37 | 35 | 35 | 29 |
+| 0.7765 | 23 | 33 | **37** | 35 | 31 |
+| 0.8265 | 23 | 35 | **37** | 35 | 33 |
+
+Two things worth reading carefully:
+
+- **Moving the racks closer is not a fix.** At 0.700 m the count collapses to 21–23 regardless of height — the arm can no longer fold back far enough to service the near slots. The relationship is not monotone and the intuition "closer is easier" is wrong here.
+- **Raising the robot is a fix,** and at the measured 0.792 m it is the only one in this table.
+
+### 3.1 The threshold
+
+Fine scan at rack distance 0.792 m:
+
+| origin_z | serviceable |
+|---|---|
+| 0.7290 | 35 / 37 |
+| 0.7315 | 35 / 37 |
+| **0.7340** | **37 / 37** |
+| 0.7365 … 0.8265 | 37 / 37 |
+
+The cliff is between **0.7315 and 0.7340** — call it **0.733 m**, i.e. **+56.5 mm** over the current build.
+
+### 3.2 Choosing the height, not just clearing the threshold
+
+Worst-case margin over all 222 poses:
+
+| origin_z | solvable | tightest pose | margin |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.6765 | 218/222 | incoming/L2/C0 lift | 11.8 mm |
+| 0.7340 | 222/222 | outgoing/L3/C0 lift | −3.8 mm |
+| 0.7515 | 222/222 | outgoing/L3/C0 lift | 5.4 mm |
+| **0.7765** | **222/222** | outgoing/L3/C0 lift | **18.1 mm** |
+| 0.8015 | 222/222 | outgoing/L3/C0 lift | 30.3 mm |
+| 0.8265 | 222/222 | outgoing/L3/C0 lift | 42.1 mm |
+| 0.8515 | 222/222 | outgoing/L3/C0 lift | 53.3 mm |
+
+Margin improves monotonically to 0.8515 — the bottom shelf (outgoing L0 at z = 0.55) never becomes binding in this range. Building at the 0.733 threshold would be building on the cliff edge. **0.7765 m** is the recommendation: comfortably clear, a round +100 mm change to the leg, and it leaves headroom for the constraints this sweep does *not* model (§5).
+
+---
+
+## 4. The near-sighting standoff is free
+
+The corrected camera geometry (findings/02 supersession) forces the close-read standoff out from 0.12 m to ≥ 0.1783 m (plate stops overhanging the tray below) or ≥ 0.2118 m (plate never enters the shelf at all). The worry was that a longer standoff pushes the flange further from the rack and costs reach.
+
+It does not:
+
+| near standoff | serviceable (origin_z 0.6765) |
+|---|---|
+| 0.1900 | 35 / 37 |
+| 0.2118 | 35 / 37 |
+| 0.2500 | 35 / 37 |
+| 0.3500 | 35 / 37 |
+
+Sighting poses are nowhere near binding — worst sighting shoulder radius is 0.7510 against a 0.9879 ceiling. **The standoff can be chosen purely on optical and collision grounds.** Choose **0.2118 m**, which keeps the plate entirely out of the shelf and still gives ~1284 px of AprilGrid width (1.65× the far read's 777 px).
+
+### 4.1 Confirmation run
+
+`origin_z = 0.7765`, near standoff `0.2118`: **37 / 37, 222 / 222.** Clean sheet, no blocked phases anywhere. (`sweep_recommended.json`)
+
+---
+
+## 5. What this sweep does *not* prove
+
+Stated plainly, because a 222/222 result invites over-reading:
+
+1. **No collision checking.** Not with the shelf plates, the rack uprights, the neighbouring jigs, the trays in adjacent slots, or the robot with itself. A pose can be perfectly reachable and still drive the elbow through a shelf. cuRobo will be the authority here and *will* reduce these counts.
+2. **No path feasibility.** Each pose is tested in isolation. Two adjacent phases can both be reachable while no collision-free path connects them — in particular if they lie in different IK branches, which would require an elbow flip inside a shelf.
+3. **No dynamics.** Carry limits (D14), joint velocity/acceleration, and singularity proximity are unmodelled. The `lift` poses are consistently the tightest, and near-singular tight poses are exactly where velocity limits bite hardest.
+4. **Table parameters are unverified.** `dist_table = 0.85`, `table_height = 0.7` are `env_config_generator` defaults, flagged as such in the code. The table slot solves comfortably, but the geometry behind that has not been confirmed against the real cell.
+5. **Nominal geometry only.** No rack registration error, no mounting tolerance. §3.2's margin is the budget those must fit inside.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Tooling kept
+
+> **Revised 2026-09-24.** `cell_geometry.py` has been retired. It was extracted
+> into `shared/cell_core` and deleted, so this section describes where its
+> contents went and what proves the move was lossless.
+
+### `shared/cell_core` — was `.agent-workspace/tools/cell_geometry.py`
+
+Single source of truth for cell geometry, and the generator for every task-phase
+flange pose. numpy-only so it stays importable on air-gapped hosts. Vendored
+into both repos with a SHA-256 manifest; each repo fails its tests if the copy
+drifts (D8).
+
+Its governing rule survived the move: **measured primitives live in `assets.py`
+and may not be hard-coded anywhere else; derived quantities are computed and
+never transcribed.** This rule exists because transcribing derived numbers
+between documents is precisely how the findings/02 camera error propagated.
+
+What was one module is now layered: `assets` (measured hardware), `config`
+(per-deployment layout, in YAML), `frames`, `geometry`, `sequence`, `scheduler`,
+`env_adapter`. The cell description that used to be module-level constants is
+now `presets/as_built.yaml`, so the numbers this document reports are a
+configuration rather than code.
+
+**The move is proven lossless.** `sweep_ported.json` was diffed against the
+pre-port `sweep.json` row by row: 222/222 rows identical in `status`, `horiz`,
+`z` and `shoulder_r`. Every number in §2–§4 therefore still describes the
+current code. The comparison tool is `.agent-workspace/tools/compare_sweeps.py`
+and it is worth keeping — a matching headline count would not have settled this,
+since two runs can agree on 218/222 while disagreeing about which 218.
+
+### `.agent-workspace/tools/reach_sweep.py`
+The sweep. CLI:
+
+```
+--urdf           default tm5s_v4.urdf
+--json           output path
+--preset         cell_core preset, default as_built
+--config         cell config YAML, overrides --preset
+--near-standoff  override the preset's close-read standoff
+--near-rise      default 0.0
+--origin-z       override the preset's mounting height
+--no-sensitivity
+```
+
+The cell now comes from a preset, so re-evaluating a changed cell is a flag
+rather than an edit. `--origin-z` and `--near-standoff` remain as overrides
+because the sensitivity scans need to sweep them freely.
+
+It strips `<visual>/<collision>/<inertial>` from the URDF into a tempfile before
+handing it to EAIK — the URDF references absolute mesh paths
+(`/ReKep-ros/rekep/configs/meshes/*.obj`) that do not exist on the program host.
+
+`.agent-workspace/tools/smoke_reach_sweep.py` exercises the geometry half with a
+stub robot, locally and without EAIK, so plumbing mistakes cost nothing instead
+of a remote round-trip.
+
+Command files: `program/.crosshost/commands/cmd_sweep_ported.sh`,
+`cmd_compare_sweeps.sh`, `cmd_reach_sweep.sh`, `cmd_scan_base.sh`,
+`cmd_scan_fine.sh`, `cmd_scan_margin.sh`, `cmd_sweep_recommended.sh`.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Decisions proposed
+
+**D19 — Robot mounting height.** Raise the robot base to `origin_z = 0.7765 m` (leg + plate stack +100 mm over the current 0.6765). Justification: the as-built height cannot service `outgoing/L3/C0` and `outgoing/L3/C5` at all, and clears its next-tightest pose by only 11.8 mm. The threshold for full coverage is 0.733 m; 0.7765 m buys 18.1 mm of worst-case margin for the collision and dynamics constraints this sweep does not model.
+
+**D20 — Rack distance stays at the measured 0.792 m.** Moving racks closer actively *hurts* (21–23 / 37 at 0.700 m). No reason to disturb a measured quantity.
+
+**D21 — Near-sighting standoff set to 0.2118 m** and chosen on optical/collision grounds alone, since reachability is provably indifferent to it across 0.19–0.35 m.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Open items
+
+1. **Confirm the base height is physically changeable** before this is treated as settled. If the 0.6765 m stack is fixed, the alternative is narrowing the outgoing rack — C0/C5 sit at ±0.43 m lateral, and they are the only failures.
+2. **Table parameters** (`dist_table`, `table_height`) still need real-cell confirmation.
+3. **Collision sweep** is the natural next validation, once `cell_core` exists and cuRobo can be pointed at the same geometry module.
+4. **Path-level check** between `pre_insert → insert → lift`, specifically whether all three hold a single IK branch.
