+# EAIK precision problem: closed-form IK returns joint solutions that miss the pose
+
+**Date:** 2026-10-21 (M6, paused)
+**Scope:** Describes the problem only: what it is, how to reproduce it, and what we measured.
+It does not propose fixes or fix directions.
+**Data and scripts:** `.agent-workspace/scratch/m6/`:
+
+- `eaik_repro_min.py`: standalone reproduction.
+- `eaik_precision_probe.py`, `eaik_precision_probe2.py`, `eaik_precision_probe3.py`, `eaik_precision_probe4.py`: measurements.
+- Each `.py` has a `.txt` output next to it.
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+For some flange poses, EAIK (`UrdfRobot.IK`) returns joint vectors whose forward kinematics
+(`fwdKin`) does not reach the requested pose. The miss can be from a few tenths of a millimetre
+up to about 90 mm (and about 0.58 rad), even though exact solutions exist close by.
+
+- The bad poses sit in thin bands. On the line we studied, the band is about 0.45 mm of flange travel.
+- Inside a band the error jumps around from one sample to the next. Two poses 2.7 µm apart can
+  differ by more than 50 mm in error.
+- In the worst samples **all 8** solutions are wrong, so no usable solution is returned.
+- `is_LS` is `True` for these solutions. It is also `True` for 99.85 % of all solutions,
+  including ones that are exact to below 1 µm.
+- The miss is not caused by noise in our inputs. The same `T` always gives the same output,
+  and `IK_batched` gives the same result as `IK`.
+
+A second, separate effect also shows up in the statistics (section 7.4). In poses where one
+wrist family cannot reach, EAIK fills those branch slots with stretched-arm (`q3 = 0`)
+approximations that miss by up to 124 mm. `is_LS` is `True` for these too.
+
+## 2. Environment
+
+| Item | Value |
+|---|---|
+| OS | Windows (local). The same EAIK is used on the program host |
+| Python | 3.10.11 |
+| eaik | 1.2.2 (`importlib.metadata.version("eaik")`) |
+| numpy | 2.1.3 |
+| Robot | Techman TM5S, `todo/resources/robot_config/tm5s_v4.urdf` (host copy: `/ReKep-ros/rekep/configs/tm5s_v4.urdf`) |
+| EAIK entry | `eaik.IK_URDF.UrdfRobot(stripped_urdf, [])` (no fixed axes) |
+| Solution object | `eaik.pybindings.EAIK.IKSolution`. Attributes: `Q`, `is_LS`, `num_solutions` (a method, not a value) |
+
+How the robot is loaded (`program/collision/check_cell.py`, `_strip_urdf` / `load_ik`):
+
+- `_strip_urdf` removes the `visual`, `collision` and `inertial` elements from every link and
+  writes a temporary URDF.
+- `load_ik` builds `UrdfRobot(tmp, [])` from that file.
+
+Stripping only removes mesh references. Kinematics are unchanged: the H/P route in section 4.2,
+which does not use the URDF, gives identical numbers.
+
+## 3. Robot model as EAIK sees it
+
+`getKinematicFamily()` = `6R-THREE_INNER_PARALLEL`, `hasKnownDecomposition()` = `True`,
+`hasSphericalWrist()` = `False`.
+
+### 3.1 Joint axes `getOriginal_H` (one row per joint, base frame, q = 0)
+
+| Joint | h_x | h_y | h_z |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
+| 2 | -3.2679488801146128e-07 | 9.9999999999994527e-01 | 5.3724798199934307e-08 |
+| 3 | same as 2 | | |
+| 4 | same as 2 | | |
+| 5 | -4.4465261963860392e-09 | 3.7307009694350584e-07 | 9.9999999999993050e-01 |
+| 6 | 8.6272668703851350e-04 | -9.9999952301508055e-01 | 4.5789985268933310e-04 |
+
+- h2, h3 and h4 are exactly parallel to each other. This is the "three inner parallel" structure.
+  They are tilted from the ideal +y by about 3.3e-7 rad.
+- h5 is tilted from +z by about 3.7e-7 rad.
+- **h6 is tilted from -y by about 0.97 mrad** (x 8.63e-4, z 4.58e-4).
+
+### 3.2 Link offsets `getOriginal_P` (one row per offset, metres)
+
+| Offset | p_x | p_y | p_z |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| p01 | 0 | 0 | 1.4840000000000000e-01 |
+| p12 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| p23 | 1.3828751071111769e-07 | -2.3047893236120272e-08 | 4.2899999999997707e-01 |
+| p34 | 1.7272680923711830e-07 | -1.4780002073772333e-01 | 3.8599999205945423e-01 |
+| p45 | -5.8469985394248116e-10 | -7.0648111527571444e-09 | 1.3149999999999984e-01 |
+| p56 | **1.1644410066328933e-04** | -1.3494989993896342e-01 | **6.1650346290020863e-05** |
+| p6T | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+
+`getRemodeled_H` is identical to `getOriginal_H`. `getRemodeled_P` differs from the original:
+
+| Offset | Remodeled value |
+|---|---|
+| p34 | [1.7272682757800064e-07, -1.4780007686125224e-01, 3.8599999205945124e-01] |
+| p45 | [0, 0, 0] |
+| p56 | [1.1644351594509451e-04, -1.3494985088024566e-01, 1.3156165034629286e-01] |
+
+In other words, EAIK has merged p45 into p56.
+
+### 3.3 Where the small terms come from (URDF)
+
+The URDF is a calibrated model. The small terms above come from these joints:
+
+```xml
+<joint name="joint_2"> <origin xyz="0. 0. 0." rpy="-1.4056475 -1.570796 -0.1651485"/>   <!-- pitch not exactly -pi/2 -->
+<joint name="joint_5"> <origin xyz="-0. -0.1315 0." rpy="1.5707959 0. -0."/>          <!-- roll not exactly pi/2 -->
+<joint name="joint_6"> <origin xyz="0.0001164 -0.1349499 0.0000616" rpy="1.5703388 0.0000002 0.0008624"/>
+<joint name="flange_fixed_joint"> <origin xyz="-0.0001164 -0.0000617 -0.0000001" rpy="0.0004576 -0.0008624 -0.0000002"/>
+```
+
+- `joint_6` is offset by 0.116 mm / 0.062 mm and turned by -0.457 / 0.862 mrad from nominal.
+  This is where the h6 tilt and the sub-millimetre p56 x/z come from.
+- `flange_fixed_joint` roughly undoes that offset and rotation for the flange frame.
+
+Joint limits (rad):
+
+| Joint | Limit (±) |
+|---|---|
+| 1 | 5.5652 |
+| 2 | 3.1416 |
+| 3 | 2.7053 |
+| 4 | 3.6652 |
+| 5 | 2.6180 |
+| 6 | 3.6652 |
+
+## 4. How to reproduce
+
+### 4.1 Minimal standalone (eaik + numpy only)
+
+`.agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_repro_min.py` (run from the repo root):
+
+```powershell
+python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_repro_min.py            # uses todo/resources/robot_config/tm5s_v4.urdf
+python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_repro_min.py <urdf>     # or another copy of the same URDF
+```
+
+The target pose, given in the EAIK base frame with the URDF flange convention, is fed straight to `IK`:
+
+```python
+T = np.array([
+    [ 1.2246467991473532e-16, 1.0, 0.0, -3.8994438991445651e-01],
+    [-1.0, 1.2246467991473532e-16, 0.0, -2.8132160922037056e-04],
+    [ 0.0, 0.0, 1.0,  1.1357087559922219e-01],
+    [ 0.0, 0.0, 0.0,  1.0]])
+sol = bot.IK(T)
+for q, ls in zip(sol.Q, sol.is_LS):
+    F = bot.fwdKin(q)
+    dp = ||F[:3,3] - T[:3,3]||
+    dr = acos((trace(T[:3,:3].T @ F[:3,:3]) - 1) / 2)
+```
+
+The script runs this twice:
+
+- **A)** `UrdfRobot` on the stripped URDF.
+- **B)** `HPRobot(H, P)` with the full-precision H/P values from section 3, so no URDF is needed.
+
+A and B print identical output (verified):
+
+```
+q [-2.522684,  1.165976,  2.483655,  2.633537,  0.999083, -3.1e-05]  is_LS True  dp 6.0672 mm  dr 47.1952 mrad
+q [-2.522684, -2.940237, -2.483655, -0.859311,  0.999083, -3.1e-05]  is_LS True  dp 6.0672 mm  dr 47.1952 mrad
+q [-0.617616,  2.948107,  2.484244,  0.850836, -0.9556,    1e-06]    is_LS True  dp 0.3047 mm  dr 2.4201 mrad
+q [-0.617616, -1.157808, -2.484244, -2.641132, -0.9556,    1e-06]    is_LS True  dp 0.3047 mm  dr 2.4201 mrad
+q [-2.522684,  0.019463,  2.58084,   0.540184, -1.000807,  3.140973] is_LS True  dp 6.3249 mm  dr 47.1953 mrad
+q [-2.522684,  2.23788,  -2.58084,  -2.799739, -1.000807,  3.140973] is_LS True  dp 6.3249 mm  dr 47.1953 mrad
+q [-0.617616, -2.244297,  2.581517,  2.805495,  0.953875, -3.140942] is_LS True  dp 0.3171 mm  dr 2.4200 mrad
+q [-0.617616, -0.025654, -2.581517, -0.533299,  0.953875, -3.140942] is_LS True  dp 0.3171 mm  dr 2.4200 mrad
+```
+
+**Expected:** every returned solution reaches T to roughly floating-point precision.
+Section 7.2 shows that exact solutions exist next to each of these 8.
+
+**Observed:** all 8 miss. The best is 0.30 mm / 2.42 mrad off.
+
+The project's tolerance is `POS_TOL = 1e-4` m and `ROT_TOL = 1e-3` rad
+([check_cell.py](../../program/collision/check_cell.py#L66-L67)).
+
+Full-precision joint vectors for this pose are in `eaik_precision_probe.txt`.
+
+### 4.2 In-repo probes
+
+```powershell
+python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_precision_probe.py   # robot dump, failing pose, fine/whole-line scans, 72-move survey (~1 min)
+python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_precision_probe2.py  # repeatability, best-branch joints along the band, is_LS stats, error split
+python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_precision_probe3.py  # geometry of the bad poses, joint-5 vs joint-1 axis
+python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_precision_probe4.py  # lateral scan through the band per parameter set
+```
+
+### 4.3 Where the pose comes from (cell context)
+
+The pose comes from the planner, `program/motion/`, preset `strict`, hub radius 0.39 m. It is on
+the `to_hub` segment of the move `incoming/L0/C3 → table/L0/C0`. The segment is a straight
+line with constant orientation, in world coordinates:
+
+| | World position (m) | Rotation |
+|---|---|---|
+| T0 | (0.339, 0.258, 0.9135) | `R = [[0,0,1],[1,0,0],[0,1,0]]` |
+| T1 (hub) | (0.39, 0, 0.8485) | same `R` |
+
+The segment is about 0.271 m long, so Δu = 1e-5 is 2.7 µm of flange travel.
+The failing sample is at u = 0.9989096061658127.
+
+World to EAIK base conversion (`check_cell.to_base`):
+
+1. `T_base = inv(base_pose) @ T_world`
+2. `T_base[:3,:3] = T_base[:3,:3] @ A.T`
+
+Here:
+
+- `base_pose` is a 180° yaw about z, at z = 0.735 m.
+- `A = R0.T @ USD_FLANGE_R0 = [[1,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0]]`, which maps the USD flange convention to the URDF flange convention.
+- `R0` is the flange rotation from `fwdKin(zeros)`.
+
+The conversion is exact and is not involved in the problem: the standalone reproduction feeds `T` straight to EAIK.
+
+## 5. How it showed up in the planner
+
+When the planner times a straight line, it solves IK at each 10 ms sample:
+[paths.py](../../program/motion/paths.py#L137) `time_line` → `nearest_branch` → `check_cell.ik_branches`.
+
+- The planner only uses IK solutions whose `fwdKin` is within `POS_TOL` / `ROT_TOL` of the pose.
+- On move 6 (`incoming/L0/C3 → table/L0/C0`), the timing sample at u = 0.99891 found no solution
+  within tolerance, so `time_line` raised `PathError("no IK near the walk at u=0.999")`.
+- The coarser path walk (2 mm steps) had passed this line without trouble, because none of its
+  samples landed inside the band.
+
+## 6. Measurement method
+
+For every raw EAIK solution q at target T (base frame):
+
+- `F = fwdKin(q)` (EAIK's own forward kinematics, so no second kinematic model is involved);
+- position error `dp = ‖F_p − T_p‖`;
+- rotation error `dr = arccos((tr(T_Rᵀ F_R) − 1) / 2)`;
+- a solution is "over tolerance" if `dp > 1e-4 m` or `dr > 1e-3 rad`.
+
+## 7. Findings
+
+### 7.1 The band on the failing line (`eaik_precision_probe.txt`)
+
+**Whole line, step 1e-4 (27 µm of travel per step)**
+
+| | Samples |
+|---|---|
+| At least one solution over tolerance | u = 0.9981, 0.9983–0.9989, 0.9991–0.9994 (12 samples) |
+| All solutions over tolerance | u = 0.9983–0.9989, 0.9992 (8 samples) |
+
+The rest of the line (u < 0.998) is clean.
+
+**Fine scan, u ∈ [0.995, 1.0], step 1e-5 (501 samples)**
+
+- 126 samples have at least one solution over tolerance. They fall in u 0.9978–0.9987, with
+  isolated samples up to the hub.
+- The errors are not smooth in u. Examples (min/max over the 8 solutions):
+
+| u | dp min (mm) | dp max (mm) | dr max (mrad) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.99848 | 0.217 | 5.00 | 37.5 |
+| 0.99853 | 0.013 | 20.8 | 147.9 |
+| 0.99857 | 0.084 | **89.9** | **563.3** |
+| 0.99860 | 1.318 | 51.9 | 343.9 |
+| 0.99866 | 0.151 | 1.00 | 7.7 |
+| 0.99867 | 0.005 | 0.82 | 6.3 |
+| 0.99868 | **3.601** | 6.70 | 49.9 |
+
+- Next to bad samples there are good ones. At u = 0.99755 and 0.99973 the error is about 0.01 mm / 0.08 mrad.
+
+**Lateral scan** (`eaik_precision_probe4.txt`)
+
+- Method: keep the failing `T` and move only its base-frame y from -5 mm to +5 mm in 2 µm steps (5,001 poses).
+- 144 poses are over tolerance (worst 99.4 mm), all with y in **[-0.578, -0.126] mm**. The hub itself is at y = 0.
+- Outside that band, no pose in ±5 mm is over tolerance.
+
+### 7.2 What is wrong in a bad solution (failing pose, u = 0.99891)
+
+Each raw solution was moved by Newton steps on `fwdKin` onto the exact solution next to it.
+Every one converged to a residual below 1e-12, so the pose is reachable in all 8 branches.
+Raw minus exact, in rad:
+
+| Raw branch | Δq1 | Δq2 | Δq3 | Δq4 | **Δq5** | Δq6 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| q1 = -2.5227, q5 = +0.999 | 0 | -8.6e-3 | +5.9e-4 | +8.0e-3 | **-4.72e-2** | +3.1e-5 |
+| q1 = -0.6176, q5 = -0.956 | 0 | +4.4e-4 | +2e-6 | -4.5e-4 | **+2.42e-3** | -2e-6 |
+| q1 = -2.5227, q5 = -1.001 | 0 | +6.5e-3 | +6.8e-4 | -7.2e-3 | **+4.72e-2** | -3.1e-5 |
+| q1 = -0.6176, q5 = +0.954 | 0 | -3.5e-4 | +2e-6 | +3.5e-4 | **-2.42e-3** | +2e-6 |
+
+(Elbow-flipped partners in the same row-pair look the same.)
+
+- q1 is exact.
+- **The q5 error equals the rotation error `dr`** (47.195 mrad and 2.420 mrad).
+- q2 and q4 are off by smaller amounts in opposite directions, and q3 and q6 are almost exact.
+- The position error follows from the rotation error: dp / dr ≈ 0.13 m, which is about the length of the last two links (p45 and p56).
+
+### 7.3 The configuration where it happens
+
+Measured on the bad solutions (`eaik_precision_probe2.txt` and `eaik_precision_probe3.txt`):
+
+**Orientation**
+
+- The flange attitude is the level "carry" attitude: base-frame `R` row 0 = [0, ±1, 0].
+- **h5 (in world) is almost parallel to h1.** The angle between them is 0.36–3.5 arcmin, which
+  is the same as saying q2 + q3 + q4 ≈ 0 or ±π (mod 2π).
+  - Along the failing line, q2 + q3 + q4 + π = +0.00112 rad and does not change with u.
+    The orientation is constant on the line, so the band is not caused by this value changing.
+- q6 ≈ 0 or ±π (within about 1 mrad).
+- |q5| ≈ 0.95–1.00 rad (rotation-bad survey: 0.949–0.962).
+
+**Position**
+
+- Base-frame x ≈ ±0.390 m and y within about 0.1–0.6 mm of 0.
+- In other words, the flange is almost in the vertical plane through joint 1 that contains the
+  flange approach direction. These are the hub ends of moves to and from `table/L0/C0`.
+
+**Not a kinematic singularity of the arm**
+
+- The smallest singular value of the 6×6 finite-difference Jacobian at the best branch stays at
+  0.1334–0.1336 across the band (0.1389 at the worst sample).
+
+**Bad poses found elsewhere** (each at the local worst u on a 1e-5 grid)
+
+| Segment | u | p_base (m) | Worst dp | Worst dr | h5∠h1 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| incoming/L0/C3 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99882 | (-0.38994, -0.000305, 0.113577) | 94.96 mm | 592.3 mrad | 3.5′ |
+| incoming/L1/C2 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99572 | (-0.390003, -0.000368, 0.239313) | 102.17 mm | 616.2 mrad | 0.36′ |
+| incoming/L2/C2 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99645 | (-0.390002, -0.000305, 0.364618) | 94.23 mm | 584.1 mrad | 0.36′ |
+| outgoing/L2/C3 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99557 | (0.390003, 0.000381, 0.229297) | 25.53 mm | 216.9 mrad | 0.41′ |
+| outgoing/L3/C3 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99645 | (0.390002, 0.000305, 0.354583) | 97.46 mm | 598.9 mrad | 0.36′ |
+
+The `from_hub` segments of the reverse moves contain the same poses.
+
+### 7.4 Survey over the whole cell (`eaik_precision_probe.txt`, `eaik_precision_probe2.txt`)
+
+**Setup**
+
+- All straight-line segments of the 72 moves in scenario `all`, hub 0.39 m.
+- Sampled every 1 mm, or every 0.25° if that gives more samples.
+- 135,956 poses; 1,078,608 raw solutions; 16 s.
