++## 4. How to reproduce
++
++### 4.1 Minimal standalone (eaik + numpy only)
++
++`.agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_repro_min.py` (run from the repo root):
++
++```powershell
++python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_repro_min.py            # uses todo/resources/robot_config/tm5s_v4.urdf
++python .agent-workspace/scratch/m6/eaik_repro_min.py <urdf>     # or another copy of the same URDF
++```
++
++The target pose, given in the EAIK base frame with the URDF flange convention, is fed straight to `IK`:
++
++```python
++T = np.array([
++    [ 1.2246467991473532e-16, 1.0, 0.0, -3.8994438991445651e-01],
++    [-1.0, 1.2246467991473532e-16, 0.0, -2.8132160922037056e-04],
++    [ 0.0, 0.0, 1.0,  1.1357087559922219e-01],
++    [ 0.0, 0.0, 0.0,  1.0]])
++sol = bot.IK(T)
++for q, ls in zip(sol.Q, sol.is_LS):
++    F = bot.fwdKin(q)
++    dp = ||F[:3,3] - T[:3,3]||
++    dr = acos((trace(T[:3,:3].T @ F[:3,:3]) - 1) / 2)
++```
++
++The script runs this twice:
++
++- **A)** `UrdfRobot` on the stripped URDF.
++- **B)** `HPRobot(H, P)` with the full-precision H/P values from section 3, so no URDF is needed.
++
++A and B print identical output (verified):
++
