++### 4.3 Where the pose comes from (cell context)
++
++The pose comes from the planner, `program/motion/`, preset `strict`, hub radius 0.39 m. It is on
++the `to_hub` segment of the move `incoming/L0/C3 → table/L0/C0`. The segment is a straight
++line with constant orientation, in world coordinates:
++
++| | World position (m) | Rotation |
++|---|---|---|
++| T0 | (0.339, 0.258, 0.9135) | `R = [[0,0,1],[1,0,0],[0,1,0]]` |
++| T1 (hub) | (0.39, 0, 0.8485) | same `R` |
++
++The segment is about 0.271 m long, so Δu = 1e-5 is 2.7 µm of flange travel.
++The failing sample is at u = 0.9989096061658127.
++
++World to EAIK base conversion (`check_cell.to_base`):
++
++1. `T_base = inv(base_pose) @ T_world`
++2. `T_base[:3,:3] = T_base[:3,:3] @ A.T`
++
++Here:
++
++- `base_pose` is a 180° yaw about z, at z = 0.735 m.
++- `A = R0.T @ USD_FLANGE_R0 = [[1,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0]]`, which maps the USD flange convention to the URDF flange convention.
++- `R0` is the flange rotation from `fwdKin(zeros)`.
++
++The conversion is exact and is not involved in the problem: the standalone reproduction feeds `T` straight to EAIK.
++
++## 5. How it showed up in the planner
++
++When the planner times a straight line, it solves IK at each 10 ms sample:
++[paths.py](../../program/motion/paths.py#L137) `time_line` → `nearest_branch` → `check_cell.ik_branches`.
++
++- The planner only uses IK solutions whose `fwdKin` is within `POS_TOL` / `ROT_TOL` of the pose.
++- On move 6 (`incoming/L0/C3 → table/L0/C0`), the timing sample at u = 0.99891 found no solution
++  within tolerance, so `time_line` raised `PathError("no IK near the walk at u=0.999")`.
++- The coarser path walk (2 mm steps) had passed this line without trouble, because none of its
++  samples landed inside the band.
++
++## 6. Measurement method
++
++For every raw EAIK solution q at target T (base frame):
++
++- `F = fwdKin(q)` (EAIK's own forward kinematics, so no second kinematic model is involved);
++- position error `dp = ‖F_p − T_p‖`;
++- rotation error `dr = arccos((tr(T_Rᵀ F_R) − 1) / 2)`;
++- a solution is "over tolerance" if `dp > 1e-4 m` or `dr > 1e-3 rad`.
++
++## 7. Findings
++
++### 7.1 The band on the failing line (`eaik_precision_probe.txt`)
++
++**Whole line, step 1e-4 (27 µm of travel per step)**
++
++| | Samples |
++|---|---|
++| At least one solution over tolerance | u = 0.9981, 0.9983–0.9989, 0.9991–0.9994 (12 samples) |
++| All solutions over tolerance | u = 0.9983–0.9989, 0.9992 (8 samples) |
++
++The rest of the line (u < 0.998) is clean.
++
++**Fine scan, u ∈ [0.995, 1.0], step 1e-5 (501 samples)**
++
++- 126 samples have at least one solution over tolerance. They fall in u 0.9978–0.9987, with
++  isolated samples up to the hub.
++- The errors are not smooth in u. Examples (min/max over the 8 solutions):
++
++| u | dp min (mm) | dp max (mm) | dr max (mrad) |
++|---|---|---|---|
++| 0.99848 | 0.217 | 5.00 | 37.5 |
++| 0.99853 | 0.013 | 20.8 | 147.9 |
++| 0.99857 | 0.084 | **89.9** | **563.3** |
++| 0.99860 | 1.318 | 51.9 | 343.9 |
++| 0.99866 | 0.151 | 1.00 | 7.7 |
++| 0.99867 | 0.005 | 0.82 | 6.3 |
++| 0.99868 | **3.601** | 6.70 | 49.9 |
++
++- Next to bad samples there are good ones. At u = 0.99755 and 0.99973 the error is about 0.01 mm / 0.08 mrad.
++
++**Lateral scan** (`eaik_precision_probe4.txt`)
++
++- Method: keep the failing `T` and move only its base-frame y from -5 mm to +5 mm in 2 µm steps (5,001 poses).
++- 144 poses are over tolerance (worst 99.4 mm), all with y in **[-0.578, -0.126] mm**. The hub itself is at y = 0.
++- Outside that band, no pose in ±5 mm is over tolerance.
++
++### 7.2 What is wrong in a bad solution (failing pose, u = 0.99891)
++
++Each raw solution was moved by Newton steps on `fwdKin` onto the exact solution next to it.
++Every one converged to a residual below 1e-12, so the pose is reachable in all 8 branches.
++Raw minus exact, in rad:
++
++| Raw branch | Δq1 | Δq2 | Δq3 | Δq4 | **Δq5** | Δq6 |
++|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
++| q1 = -2.5227, q5 = +0.999 | 0 | -8.6e-3 | +5.9e-4 | +8.0e-3 | **-4.72e-2** | +3.1e-5 |
++| q1 = -0.6176, q5 = -0.956 | 0 | +4.4e-4 | +2e-6 | -4.5e-4 | **+2.42e-3** | -2e-6 |
++| q1 = -2.5227, q5 = -1.001 | 0 | +6.5e-3 | +6.8e-4 | -7.2e-3 | **+4.72e-2** | -3.1e-5 |
++| q1 = -0.6176, q5 = +0.954 | 0 | -3.5e-4 | +2e-6 | +3.5e-4 | **-2.42e-3** | +2e-6 |
++
++(Elbow-flipped partners in the same row-pair look the same.)
++
++- q1 is exact.
++- **The q5 error equals the rotation error `dr`** (47.195 mrad and 2.420 mrad).
++- q2 and q4 are off by smaller amounts in opposite directions, and q3 and q6 are almost exact.
++- The position error follows from the rotation error: dp / dr ≈ 0.13 m, which is about the length of the last two links (p45 and p56).
++
++### 7.3 The configuration where it happens
++
++Measured on the bad solutions (`eaik_precision_probe2.txt` and `eaik_precision_probe3.txt`):
++
++**Orientation**
++
++- The flange attitude is the level "carry" attitude: base-frame `R` row 0 = [0, ±1, 0].
++- **h5 (in world) is almost parallel to h1.** The angle between them is 0.36–3.5 arcmin, which
++  is the same as saying q2 + q3 + q4 ≈ 0 or ±π (mod 2π).
++  - Along the failing line, q2 + q3 + q4 + π = +0.00112 rad and does not change with u.
++    The orientation is constant on the line, so the band is not caused by this value changing.
++- q6 ≈ 0 or ±π (within about 1 mrad).
++- |q5| ≈ 0.95–1.00 rad (rotation-bad survey: 0.949–0.962).
++
++**Position**
++
++- Base-frame x ≈ ±0.390 m and y within about 0.1–0.6 mm of 0.
++- In other words, the flange is almost in the vertical plane through joint 1 that contains the
++  flange approach direction. These are the hub ends of moves to and from `table/L0/C0`.
++
++**Not a kinematic singularity of the arm**
++
++- The smallest singular value of the 6×6 finite-difference Jacobian at the best branch stays at
++  0.1334–0.1336 across the band (0.1389 at the worst sample).
++
++**Bad poses found elsewhere** (each at the local worst u on a 1e-5 grid)
++
++| Segment | u | p_base (m) | Worst dp | Worst dr | h5∠h1 |
++|---|---|---|---|---|---|
++| incoming/L0/C3 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99882 | (-0.38994, -0.000305, 0.113577) | 94.96 mm | 592.3 mrad | 3.5′ |
++| incoming/L1/C2 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99572 | (-0.390003, -0.000368, 0.239313) | 102.17 mm | 616.2 mrad | 0.36′ |
++| incoming/L2/C2 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99645 | (-0.390002, -0.000305, 0.364618) | 94.23 mm | 584.1 mrad | 0.36′ |
++| outgoing/L2/C3 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99557 | (0.390003, 0.000381, 0.229297) | 25.53 mm | 216.9 mrad | 0.41′ |
++| outgoing/L3/C3 → table/L0/C0 to_hub | 0.99645 | (0.390002, 0.000305, 0.354583) | 97.46 mm | 598.9 mrad | 0.36′ |
++
++The `from_hub` segments of the reverse moves contain the same poses.
