++EAIK's usual noise level in this cell (up to about 0.04 mm / 0.3 mrad). Example: along the
++failing line just before the band, the best branch is off by 0.001–0.05 mm.
++
++The 9,092 over-tolerance solutions fall into two different kinds:
++
++1. **Rotation is wrong too (`dr` > 1 mrad): 40 solutions.**
++   - This is the problem in 7.1–7.3.
++   - All of them are in `to_hub` / `from_hub` segments of moves to and from `table/L0/C0`, at
++     u ≈ 0.995–0.997 from the hub.
++   - A 1 mm grid only lands in a band of about 0.45 mm by chance. The failing
++     `incoming/L0/C3` band was **not** hit by this survey, so the count of affected moves is a lower bound.
++2. **Position only (`dr` ≈ 0, `dp` up to 123.9 mm): 9,052 solutions.**
++   - All are at `q3 = 0` (arm fully stretched), for example
++     `q = [-0.4478, 0.8185, 0.0, 2.322, -2.0203, -3.1412]` at `outgoing/L3/C0` lift / withdraw end
++     (123.890 mm off).
++   - They occur only in the insert, lift, withdraw, enter, lower, out, arrive and retreat segments of
++     `incoming/L2/*` and `outgoing/L3/*` ↔ `table/L0/C0`.
++   - These are the poses where one wrist family is out of reach (see the reach findings, 04 and 06).
++   - Each such pose still has exact solutions in the other branches. For example, at
++     `outgoing/L3/C0` lift u = 1, 4 solutions are off by 0.000 mm and 2 by 123.85 / 123.89 mm.
++     The distance from the joint-2 axis there is 0.959 m.
++   - So EAIK fills the unreachable branches with stretched-arm approximations.
++
++### 7.5 The `is_LS` flag
++
++| | Solutions |
++|---|---|
++| `is_LS = True` | 1,077,016 (99.85 %) |
++| `is_LS = False` | 1,592 |
++| Over tolerance with `is_LS = True` | 9,092 |
++| Over tolerance with `is_LS = False` | 0 |
++
++- At a clean pose (u = 0.5 on the failing line) all 8 solutions have `is_LS = True` and dp = 0.000 mm.
++- So, on this robot, the flag does not tell a bad solution from a good one.
++- Code note: `check_cell.ik_branches` reads `getattr(sol, "is_LS_vec", [False]*n)`
++  ([check_cell.py](../../program/collision/check_cell.py#L304)). EAIK 1.2.2 has no `is_LS_vec`
++  attribute, so this lookup always returns the all-`False` default and no solution is ever skipped by it.
++
++### 7.6 Determinism
++
++- Calling `IK(T)` twice with the same `T` gives bit-identical `Q`.
++- `IK_batched([T])[0].Q` equals `IK(T).Q`.
++
++### 7.7 Sensitivity to the small calibration terms (diagnostic only)
++
++Method:
++
++- Build `HPRobot(H, P)` with parts of the small terms from section 3 set to their nominal values.
++- Use the same target `T` and the same ±5 mm lateral scan as in 7.1.
++- A wide scan is needed because changing the parameters can move the band.
++
++| H | P | Worst dp in scan | Over-tol poses | y band (mm) |
++|---|---|---|---|---|
++| URDF | URDF | 99.379 mm | 144 | -0.578 … -0.126 |
++| all axes exact (rounded) | URDF | 0.000 | 0 | none |
++| URDF | all components < 1 mm set to 0 | 0.000 | 0 | none |
++| only h6 tilted (h1–h5 exact) | URDF | 95.330 mm | 167 | -0.582 … -0.094 |
++| only h6 tilted | only p56 x/z kept (other < 1 mm terms 0) | 99.019 mm | 151 | -0.590 … -0.094 |
++| URDF | only p56 x/z set to 0 | 0.000 | 0 | none |
++
++In this scan, the problem appears only when **both** of these are present:
++
++- the h6 tilt (about 0.97 mrad);
++- the sub-millimetre p56 x/z offsets (0.116 mm, 0.062 mm).
++
++The tiny tilts of h2–h5 (below 4e-7 rad) are not needed. All variants keep the same kinematic
++family `6R-THREE_INNER_PARALLEL`.
++
