++# EAIK returns IK solutions that miss reachable poses: standalone numerical reproduction
++
++This page is self-contained. It needs only Python, `numpy` and `eaik`.
++
++- The robot model is a kinematics-only URDF, included below.
++- All target poses are written out as numbers.
++- It describes the problem only. It does not propose fixes.
++
++## 1. Problem in one paragraph
++
++For a 6R arm with three parallel inner axes (EAIK family `6R-THREE_INNER_PARALLEL`), some
++target poses are clearly reachable, yet `IK(T)` returns joint vectors `q` where
++`fwdKin(q)` misses `T`:
++
++- the miss ranges from 0.3 mm up to about 100 mm in position, and up to 0.57 rad in rotation;
++- at some poses **every** returned solution misses;
++- every returned solution has `is_LS = True`, good or bad;
++- an exact solution exists next to each bad one (Newton on `fwdKin` reaches residual ~1e-16).
++
++The bad poses form a band about 0.45 mm wide. Inside the band the error changes erratically
++from one pose to the next, even when the poses are 2 µm apart.
++
++## 2. Environment used
++
++| Item | Value |
++|---|---|
++| Python | 3.10.11 (Windows) |
++| eaik | 1.2.2 |
++| numpy | 2.1.3 |
++
++```
++pip install eaik==1.2.2 numpy
++```
++
++## 3. Robot model (kinematics only)
++
++The robot is a Techman TM5S with calibrated values (`tm5s_v4.urdf`). Only the joint chain is
++kept; links have no meshes or inertia. The model has these small calibration terms:
++
++| Term | Size | Effect |
++|---|---|---|
++| `joint_2` rpy | pitch ≈ -π/2 (with roll/yaw) | h2–h4 tilted from +y by about 3e-7 rad |
++| `joint_5` rpy | roll 1.5707959 (≈ π/2) | h5 tilted from +z by about 4e-7 rad |
++| `joint_6` xyz | 0.1164 mm, 0.0616 mm off the nominal axis | p56 has sub-mm x/z |
++| `joint_6` rpy | 1.5703388 (π/2 − 0.457 mrad), yaw 0.8624 mrad | **h6 tilted by about 0.97 mrad** |
++| `flange_fixed_joint` | about the inverse of the joint_6 offset | flange frame back near nominal |
++
++How EAIK reads the model, using `getOriginal_H` / `getOriginal_P` (base frame, q = 0):
++
++```
++h1 = [ 0,           0,          1          ]
++h2 = h3 = h4 = [-3.268e-07, 0.99999999999994527, 5.372e-08]
++h5 = [-4.447e-09,   3.731e-07,  0.99999999999993050]
++h6 = [ 8.6272668703851350e-04, -9.9999952301508055e-01, 4.5789985268933310e-04]
++
++p01 = [0, 0, 0.1484]          p12 = 0
++p23 = [1.38e-07, -2.30e-08, 0.429]
++p34 = [1.73e-07, -0.14780002, 0.38599999]
++p45 = [-5.8e-10, -7.1e-09, 0.1315]
++p56 = [1.1644410066328933e-04, -1.3494989993896342e-01, 6.1650346290020863e-05]
++p6T = 0
++```
++
++`hasSphericalWrist()` = False and `hasKnownDecomposition()` = True.
++
++`getRemodeled_P` merges p45 into p56, giving p56 = [1.16444e-4, -0.13494985, 0.13156165], p45 = 0.
++`getRemodeled_H` equals the original H.
++
++## 4. Reproduction script
++
++Save the script as `eaik_standalone_repro.py` and run `python eaik_standalone_repro.py`. It takes about a minute.
++
++Every target uses the same base-frame orientation `R = [[0,1,0],[-1,0,0],[0,0,1]]`, which keeps
++the flange level. Only Example 6 uses a different orientation.
++
++Error measures:
++
++- `dp = ‖fwdKin(q)_p − T_p‖`
++- `dr = arccos((tr(T_Rᵀ fwdKin(q)_R) − 1)/2)`
++
++Both use EAIK's own `fwdKin`, so no second kinematic model is involved.
++
++```python
++"""EAIK precision repro: standalone (needs only numpy + eaik)."""
++import math
++import os
++import tempfile
++
++import numpy as np
++from eaik.IK_HP import HPRobot
++from eaik.IK_URDF import UrdfRobot
++
++np.set_printoptions(precision=6, suppress=True, linewidth=200)
++
++URDF = """<?xml version="1.0"?>
++<robot name="tm5s_kinematics">
++  <link name="link_0"/> <link name="link_1"/> <link name="link_2"/> <link name="link_3"/>
++  <link name="link_4"/> <link name="link_5"/> <link name="link_6"/> <link name="flange"/>
++  <joint name="joint_1" type="revolute">
++    <origin xyz="0. 0. 0.1484" rpy="0. 0. 0."/>
++    <parent link="link_0"/> <child link="link_1"/> <axis xyz="0. 0. 1."/>
++    <limit lower="-5.565191429188092" upper="5.565191429188092" effort="170." velocity="3.6651914"/>
++  </joint>
++  <joint name="joint_2" type="revolute">
++    <origin xyz="0. 0. 0." rpy="-1.4056475 -1.570796 -0.1651485"/>
++    <parent link="link_1"/> <child link="link_2"/> <axis xyz="0. 0. 1."/>
++    <limit lower="-3.1415927" upper="3.1415927" effort="170." velocity="3.6651914"/>
++  </joint>
++  <joint name="joint_3" type="revolute">
++    <origin xyz="0.429 -0. 0. " rpy="-0. -0. -0."/>
++    <parent link="link_2"/> <child link="link_3"/> <axis xyz="0. 0. 1."/>
++    <limit lower="-2.705260340591211" upper="2.705260340591211" effort="170." velocity="3.6651914"/>
++  </joint>
++  <joint name="joint_4" type="revolute">
++    <origin xyz="0.386 -0. -0.1478" rpy="0. 0. 1.570796"/>
++    <parent link="link_3"/> <child link="link_4"/> <axis xyz="0. 0. 1."/>
++    <limit lower="-3.665191429188092" upper="3.665191429188092" effort="45." velocity="3.9269908"/>
++  </joint>
++  <joint name="joint_5" type="revolute">
++    <origin xyz="-0. -0.1315 0. " rpy="1.5707959 0. -0. "/>
++    <parent link="link_4"/> <child link="link_5"/> <axis xyz="0. 0. 1."/>
++    <limit lower="-2.6179938779914944" upper="2.6179938779914944" effort="45." velocity="3.9269908"/>
++  </joint>
++  <joint name="joint_6" type="revolute">
++    <origin xyz="0.0001164 -0.1349499 0.0000616" rpy="1.5703388 0.0000002 0.0008624"/>
++    <parent link="link_5"/> <child link="link_6"/> <axis xyz="0. 0. 1."/>
++    <limit lower="-3.665191429188092" upper="3.665191429188092" effort="45." velocity="7.8539816"/>
++  </joint>
++  <joint name="flange_fixed_joint" type="fixed">
++    <origin xyz="-0.0001164 -0.0000617 -0.0000001" rpy="0.0004576 -0.0008624 -0.0000002"/>
++    <parent link="link_6"/> <child link="flange"/>
++  </joint>
++</robot>
++"""
++
++
++def load():
++    fd, path = tempfile.mkstemp(suffix=".urdf")
++    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
++        f.write(URDF)
++    return UrdfRobot(path, [])
++
++
++def pose(x, y, z):
++    """Flange pose in the base frame; the orientation is the same for every example."""
++    return np.array([[0.0, 1.0, 0.0, x],
++                     [-1.0, 0.0, 0.0, y],
++                     [0.0, 0.0, 1.0, z],
++                     [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]])
++
++
++def errors(bot, T, q):
++    F = np.asarray(bot.fwdKin(q), dtype=float)
++    dp = float(np.linalg.norm(F[:3, 3] - T[:3, 3]))
++    c = (np.trace(T[:3, :3].T @ F[:3, :3]) - 1.0) / 2.0
++    return dp, math.acos(max(-1.0, min(1.0, c)))
++
++
++def show(bot, T, label):
++    sol = bot.IK(T)
++    print(f"\n{label}: p = {T[:3, 3].tolist()}  ({len(sol.Q)} solutions)")
++    for q, ls in zip(sol.Q, sol.is_LS):
++        dp, dr = errors(bot, T, q)
++        print(f"  q = {np.asarray(q)}"
++              f"  is_LS={bool(ls)}  dp = {dp*1e3:9.4f} mm  dr = {dr*1e3:8.4f} mrad")
++
++
++def newton(bot, T, q, iters=20):
++    """Plain Newton on fwdKin, only to show an exact solution exists next to q."""
++    q = np.asarray(q, dtype=float).copy()
++
++    def res(Ta, Tb):
++        R = Ta[:3, :3] @ Tb[:3, :3].T
++        w = 0.5 * np.array([R[2, 1] - R[1, 2], R[0, 2] - R[2, 0], R[1, 0] - R[0, 1]])
++        return np.concatenate([Ta[:3, 3] - Tb[:3, 3], w])
++
++    for _ in range(iters):
++        F = np.asarray(bot.fwdKin(q), dtype=float)
++        r = res(T, F)
++        J = np.column_stack([res(np.asarray(bot.fwdKin(q + 1e-7 * e), dtype=float), F) / 1e-7
++                             for e in np.eye(6)])
++        q = q + np.linalg.lstsq(J, r, rcond=None)[0]
++    return q
++
++
++if __name__ == "__main__":
++    bot = load()
++    print("eaik family:", bot.getKinematicFamily())
++
++    # Example 1: one pose, every solution misses
++    T = pose(-0.38994438991445651, -2.8132160922037056e-04, 0.11357087559922219)
++    show(bot, T, "Example 1")
++    print("  raw -> nearest exact solution (Newton on fwdKin):")
++    for q in bot.IK(T).Q:
++        qe = newton(bot, T, q)
++        dp, dr = errors(bot, T, qe)
++        print(f"    dq = {qe - np.asarray(q)}"
++              f"  after: dp {dp:.1e} m  dr {dr:.1e} rad")
++
++    # Example 2: a pose 29 um away, much larger miss
++    show(bot, pose(-0.38992707, -0.00036894, 0.11359295), "Example 2")
++
++    # Example 3: straight line, constant orientation; the miss appears only in a thin band
++    p0, p1 = np.array([-0.339, -0.258, 0.1785]), np.array([-0.39, 0.0, 0.1135])
++    print("\nExample 3: line p0 -> p1, u in [0.995, 1], step 1e-5 (2.7 um)")
++    worst, n_bad = [], 0
++    for u in np.linspace(0.995, 1.0, 501):
++        T = pose(*(p0 + u * (p1 - p0)))
++        e = [errors(bot, T, q) for q in bot.IK(T).Q]
++        wp = max(x[0] for x in e)
++        bp = min(x[0] for x in e)
++        worst.append((wp, u))
++        if wp > 1e-4:
++            n_bad += 1
++            if wp > 0.01 or bp > 1e-4:
++                print(f"  u {u:.5f}  best dp {bp*1e3:8.4f} mm  worst dp {wp*1e3:8.4f} mm")
++    print(f"  samples with any solution > 0.1 mm: {n_bad} / 501;"
++          f" worst {max(worst)[0]*1e3:.2f} mm at u {max(worst)[1]:.5f}")
++
++    # Example 4: lateral scan through the band at the line's end point
++    def lateral(robot, label):
++        bad = []
++        for y in np.linspace(-0.005, 0.005, 5001):
++            T = pose(-0.39, y, 0.1135)
++            wp = max(errors(robot, T, q)[0] for q in robot.IK(T).Q)
++            if wp > 1e-4:
++                bad.append((y, wp))
++        if bad:
++            print(f"  {label:52s} {len(bad):4d} poses over 0.1 mm, y in [{bad[0][0]*1e3:+.3f}, "
++                  f"{bad[-1][0]*1e3:+.3f}] mm, worst {max(b[1] for b in bad)*1e3:7.2f} mm")
++        else:
++            print(f"  {label:52s}    0 poses over 0.1 mm")
++
++    print("\nExample 4: p = (-0.39, y, 0.1135), y in [-5, +5] mm, step 2 um")
++    lateral(bot, "UrdfRobot")
++
++    # Example 5: same scan with HPRobot built from EAIK's own H/P, with small terms set to nominal
++    H = np.asarray(bot.getOriginal_H(), dtype=float).T   # (6, 3) joint axes
++    P = np.asarray(bot.getOriginal_P(), dtype=float).T   # (7, 3) offsets
++    H6 = np.round(H)
++    H6[5] = H[5]                                          # only h6 keeps its tilt
++    Psmall0 = np.where(np.abs(P) < 1e-3, 0.0, P)          # every sub-mm component -> 0
++    Pkeep56 = Psmall0.copy()
++    Pkeep56[5] = P[5]                                     # ... except p56
++    Pno56 = P.copy()
++    Pno56[5] = Psmall0[5]                                 # only p56 x/z -> 0
++    print("\nExample 5: Example 4 scan, HPRobot(H, P) variants")
++    for label, Hs, Ps in [("H, P as EAIK reads them", H, P),
++                          ("all axes exact, P as read", np.round(H), P),
++                          ("H as read, all sub-mm P components = 0", H, Psmall0),
++                          ("only h6 tilted, P as read", H6, P),
++                          ("only h6 tilted, only p56 x/z kept", H6, Pkeep56),
++                          ("H as read, only p56 x/z = 0", H, Pno56)]:
++        lateral(HPRobot(Hs, Ps), label)
++
++    # Example 6: separate effect, stretched-arm (q3 = 0) fill-ins
++    show(bot, pose(0.689, -0.43, 0.6585) @ np.diag([-1.0, -1.0, 1.0, 1.0]), "Example 6")
++```
